what moviegoers find scary differs  dramatically from person to person but  everyone can agree that some horror  movie tropes are totally played out from  zombies to creepy kids to haunted dolls  these are the horror movie staples that  should be laid to rest there are plenty  of terrifying movie monsters out there  but which one perfectly combines extreme  gore and mindless violence into one  flesh-eating package I'm thinking  zombies that's right however something  strange has happened to these undead  villains in the 21st century zombies  just aren't as scary as they used to be  zombies lost a good chunk of their bite  in the 2000s with films like zombie land  and Shaun of the Dead which deliberately  take all the gravitas out of the Walking  Dead zombies became downright funny and  were also portrayed as increasingly easy  to take out unlike other supernatural  creatures you don't need silver holy  water or wooden stakes to dispatch a  zombie as long as you've got a couple of  long-range weapons and you're smart  about how much noise you're making  you could probably live indefinitely in  the zombie apocalypse who knows you  might even have time to develop your  very own undead transportation system  really a lot of zombie movies these days  tend to be blood-soaked comedies or  action flicks rather than outright  horror films the creatures just amble  around while armed civilians pick them  off one by one or use them as visual  punchlines many of these movies are  seriously entertaining but they're not  even close to being frightening Night of  the Living Dead truly terrified  audiences when it came out in 1968  however more recent movies like Pride  and Prejudice and Zombies and little  monsters probably won't make you scream  but they might make you laugh or even  cheer  creepy kids are an understandably  popular horror trope children are  supposed to exemplify innocence so to  see that purity tainted by say a demon  is definitely disturbing from Damien in  the omen to the twins in the shining  kids have been extraordinary tools of  terror for decades but this storytelling  device lost a whole lot of power in  recent years the thing about creepy kids  is that well they're still kids they  have the potential to be seriously scary  if a scene is done right but if it's not  things can get silly really fast  also some modern horror films seem to  assume the audience will be frightened  of any child doing commonplace things as  long as those moments are punctuated by  a loud music cue then there's the  tendency of filmmakers to randomly wedge  a strange kid into a movie just to add  an extra bit of atmosphere or whatever  this is  discovering that a young character is  capable of supernatural mayhem used to  be a novelty these days it's so overdone  that it would be more shocking to see a  normal kid who is under the influence of  sugar rather than an ancient curse plus  even in classic horror films it just  feels wrong to root against children who  have gone to the dark side especially  when their adversaries are so much  bigger than they are films like  Rosemary's Baby terrified the world with  visions of about Satanists these days  however if you hear someone as a  Satanist  you might just laugh it off most  people's experiences with Satanists  involve heavy metal bands with lots of  makeup and awesome videos fans of these  bands may like the aesthetic of inverted  crosses and pentagrams but no one really  expects them to sacrifice goats let  alone people the Church of Satan's  website makes it clear that the members  are more interested in individual  freedom than literal devil worship  additionally documentaries like hail  Satan have revealed that real-world  Satanists aren't very scary and  open-mindedness and free expression all  of this means one thing for movies  Satanists just aren't that ominous  anymore no one's worried about them  roaming around their neighborhood and  robes looking for recruits there are a  lot of scary things in the real world  but generally speaking devil worshipers  aren't at the top of this list the Blair  Witch Project revolutionized horror  films not only is it genuinely  suspenseful in frightening it had a  totally unique marketing campaign  through the use of missing-person  leaflets and online message boards the  Blair Witch Project initially managed to  convince thousands of people that the  film was totally real and composed of  actual footage even today when everyone  knows the truth the raw found footage  nature of the film manages to impress  however the success of the movie along  with its rock bottom production costs  led to an overabundance of found footage  films and of course the quality and  impact of this sub-genre slowly  dissipated over time Plus no matter how  hard these movies try to convince  viewers that what they're seeing is  totally real audiences have become a  whole lot savvy ER all it takes is a  quick google search to show that a film  is not in fact based on a true story  those days are long gone the other issue  is that found-footage movies started to  feel a bit repetitive and loaded with  cliches like night vision jump-scare  that's not the safe found footage films  can't be great but filmmakers need to  find a unique angle on them much like  the Blair Witch Project did all those  years ago Chucky was far from the first  evil doll in cinema but he quickly  became the most popular since child's  play was released in 1988 haunted dolls  have cropped up in countless horror  movies including a never-ending string  of Chucky films whether it's Annabel and  the conjuring or Billy the puppet in the  Saw franchise there's something  unsettling about seeing a doll a puppet  or any child's toy used for nefarious  purposes however despite the  surprisingly high number of movies that  incorporate an evil doll you're more  likely to chuckle from their antics than  screamin horror at the end of the day  it's just hard to be afraid of something  you could toss in a toy box  sure the dolls usually have supernatural  abilities but a lot of movies don't  explore this very thoroughly featuring a  demonic doll in a film is just a fun  excuse to have a series of sight gags of  them attacking humans with their tiny  limbs sure it would be a shock to see a  Cabbage Patch Kid hopping towards you  with the power to land a murderous gleam  in its eye but it's probably not going  to give you any nightmares at the other  end of the spectrum there's the Haunted  doll that basically just sits there and  occasionally moves its eyes again at  some point this was probably terrifying  but now it just seems like the cheapest  possible way to make a horror movie  still the fact remains that studios are  constantly pumping out new movies with  cursed dolls and puppets so there's  clearly an audience for these films and  somebody is making plenty of money from  them now  who's the dummy check out one of our  newest videos right here plus even more  looper videos about horror films are  coming soon subscribe to our YouTube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]
 

